    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>From 1902 to 1934 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a>, written into both Cuban and American law, gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the right to intervene in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, restricted its foreign relations and leased it the naval base at Guantánamo Bay.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> ruled its politics: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> boom and the 1920 crash known as the Dance of the Millions ruined the country and handed more power to foreign investors, and by 1959 American firms owned about 40 per cent of the sugar land, 90 per cent of the mines and 80 per cent of the utilities.</li><li>The 1940 constitution wrote the right to work and to health care into law, but after his coup of 1952 Batista suspended it, and his secret police killed between hundreds and about 20,000 people before he fled in December 1958.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> became the first country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> to broadcast television, and by the late 1950s it had the ninth highest number of television sets and the fourth highest number of channels of any country in the world.</p></aside>
    </section>

